  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A water spot on the ceiling, a few shingles in the yard after a windy night, or a home inspector's note during a sale can all lead to the same question: fix it, or replace the whole roof? The roof repair vs replacement decision is not always obvious, and the wrong call either way costs money. Patch a roof that is worn out and you may pay for several repairs before replacing it anyway. Replace a roof that only needed a targeted fix and you have spent far more than necessary. This guide gives Temple homeowners a practical way to think it through.</p>

<h2>Start With Three Questions</h2>
<p>Most experienced roofers boil the decision down to three things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>How old is the roof, and how much life does it realistically have left?</strong></li>
<li><strong>Is the problem isolated, or is it a symptom of something widespread?</strong></li>
<li><strong>What is the condition of the layers you cannot see, like the decking and underlayment?</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>If the roof is relatively young, the damage is confined to one area, and the structure underneath is sound, repair is usually the smart move. If the roof is near the end of its service life, problems are showing up in several places, or the decking is compromised, replacement tends to make more sense. Most real-world cases land somewhere in between, which is where the details matter.</p>

<h2>When Roof Repair Makes Sense</h2>
<p>Many roof problems are local and very fixable. A <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> is often the right answer when:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A few shingles blew off</strong> in a single wind event and the rest of the roof is in good shape.</li>
<li><strong>A pipe boot has cracked.</strong> The rubber collars around plumbing vents commonly dry out and split in our summer heat long before the shingles wear out. Replacing them is a small job that stops a lot of leaks.</li>
<li><strong>Flashing has failed</strong> at a chimney, skylight, or wall, while the field shingles are fine.</li>
<li><strong>A tree limb caused damage</strong> to one section.</li>
<li><strong>Exposed nail heads or a lifted ridge cap</strong> are letting water in at a specific spot.</li>
</ul>
<p>In these cases the leak has a clear cause, and fixing that cause restores the roof's performance. A good repair matches materials as closely as possible and seals the area correctly, rather than smearing roofing cement over the problem.</p>

<h3>The matching problem</h3>
<p>One complication with repairs on asphalt roofs is matching. Shingle colors fade, and product lines get discontinued. On a roof that is only a few years old, a close match is usually possible. On an older roof, new shingles may stand out. That is mostly a cosmetic issue, but it is worth knowing before you decide, especially on the street-facing side of the house.</p>

<h2>When Roof Replacement Is the Better Investment</h2>
<p>Replacement starts to make more sense when the problems are not really about one spot, but about the roof as a whole. Signs pointing toward a <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a> include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Widespread granule loss</strong>, with shingles looking bald or shiny over large areas.</li>
<li><strong>Curling, cupping, or cracking</strong> across multiple slopes, which is common on older roofs that have endured years of Central Texas sun.</li>
<li><strong>Repeated leaks in different places</strong>, which suggests the whole system is breaking down.</li>
<li><strong>Sagging rooflines or soft spots in the decking</strong>, which point to long-term moisture damage.</li>
<li><strong>Widespread hail or wind damage</strong> across several slopes after a major storm.</li>
<li><strong>Two existing layers</strong>, where adding repairs on top of an already heavy, aging system does not make long-term sense.</li>
</ul>
<p>Age matters too. Asphalt shingle roofs in hot climates often wear out sooner than the number printed on the package. If your roof is approaching the end of its expected life, money spent on repairs buys less time. Our article on <a href="/blog/how-long-does-a-roof-last-temple-texas/">how long a roof lasts in Temple</a> covers typical lifespans for different materials.</p>

<h2>Roof Repair vs Replacement at a Glance</h2>
<p>This side-by-side comparison summarizes the trade-offs most homeowners weigh.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Repair</th><th>Replacement</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Isolated damage on a roof with good remaining life</td><td>Aging roofs, widespread damage, or recurring leaks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upfront cost</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
<tr><td>Long-term value</td><td>Good if the rest of the roof is sound</td><td>Resets the clock with new materials and underlayment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time on site</td><td>Often a few hours</td><td>Commonly one to three days for a typical home</td></tr>
<tr><td>Decking inspection</td><td>Only the repaired area</td><td>The entire deck is exposed and can be fixed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Appearance</td><td>New shingles may not match older ones</td><td>Uniform look across the whole roof</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upgrade opportunity</td><td>Limited</td><td>Can add impact-resistant shingles, metal, better ventilation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Effect on home sale</td><td>Buyers may still ask about roof age</td><td>A new roof is often a selling point</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

